    "['backward in-label [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnels', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For the HUAWEI command `backward in-label 20`, the equivalent Cisco command (for MPLS-TE static bidirectional LSP configuration) would be:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng tunnels\\ninterface Tunnel10\\n  mpls traffic-eng bidirectional static lsp association ingress in-label 20\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's syntax for static bidirectional LSPs is more verbose and requires enabling MPLS TE globally first. The exact implementation may vary depending on Cisco IOS version. This translation assumes the backward (return path) label mapping for a static CR-LSP.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnels'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface Tunnel10', 'parent_command': ['mpls traffic-eng tunnel [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnel [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For the HUAWEI command `backward in-label 20`, the equivalent Cisco command (for MPLS-TE static bidirectional LSP configuration) would be:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng tunnels\\ninterface Tunnel10\\n  mpls traffic-eng bidirectional static lsp association ingress in-label 20\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's syntax for static bidirectional LSPs is more verbose and requires enabling MPLS TE globally first. The exact implementation may vary depending on Cisco IOS version. This translation assumes the backward (return path) label mapping for a static CR-LSP.\", 'trans_template': 'interface Tunnel10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng bidirectional static lsp association ingress in-label 20', 'parent_command': ['mpls traffic-eng tunnel [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnel [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For the HUAWEI command `backward in-label 20`, the equivalent Cisco command (for MPLS-TE static bidirectional LSP configuration) would be:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng tunnels\\ninterface Tunnel10\\n  mpls traffic-eng bidirectional static lsp association ingress in-label 20\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's syntax for static bidirectional LSPs is more verbose and requires enabling MPLS TE globally first. The exact implementation may vary depending on Cisco IOS version. This translation assumes the backward (return path) label mapping for a static CR-LSP.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng bidirectional static lsp association ingress in-label 20'}]]": 1,
    "['pw-template [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'l2vpn pw-class tpatoc', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'l2vpn pw-class tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nl2vpn pw-class tpatoc\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Huawei, `pw-template` is used to define a Pseudowire (PW) template for L2VPN services.\\n- The equivalent Cisco command is `l2vpn pw-class`, which serves the same purpose of creating a pseudowire class template.\\n- Both commands establish the base configuration for pseudowire parameters that can be referenced by L2VPN services.', 'trans_template': 'l2vpn pw-class tpatoc'}]]": 1,
    "['peer-address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'peer-address 3.3.3.3', 'parent_command': ['l2vpn pw-class tpatoc'], 'root': 'l2vpn pw-class tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\npeer-address 3.3.3.3\\n##\\n\\nFor Cisco devices, the equivalent command would be:\\n##\\nneighbor 3.3.3.3\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact context in Cisco may vary depending on the feature being configured (e.g., LDP, BGP, or MPLS L2VPN). In an MPLS L2VPN pseudowire context, Cisco typically uses the `neighbor` command to specify the peer address.', 'trans_template': 'peer-address 3.3.3.3'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3', 'parent_command': ['l2vpn pw-class tpatoc'], 'root': 'l2vpn pw-class tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\npeer-address 3.3.3.3\\n##\\n\\nFor Cisco devices, the equivalent command would be:\\n##\\nneighbor 3.3.3.3\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact context in Cisco may vary depending on the feature being configured (e.g., LDP, BGP, or MPLS L2VPN). In an MPLS L2VPN pseudowire context, Cisco typically uses the `neighbor` command to specify the peer address.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3'}]]": 1,
