    "['mpls te reserved-for-binding', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnels', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nmpls traffic-eng tunnels\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng reserved-for-binding\\n##\\n\\nThe `mpls te reserved-for-binding` command in Huawei specifies that the TE tunnel is reserved for binding to other services (like L2VPN). In Cisco, the equivalent is to first enable MPLS TE globally (`mpls traffic-eng tunnels`), then configure the tunnel-specific reservation using `tunnel mpls traffic-eng reserved-for-binding`. \\n\\nNote: Cisco's exact implementation may vary slightly depending on IOS/IOS-XE version, and some contexts may require additional parameters. The above is the closest functional equivalent.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnels'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng reserved-for-binding', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nmpls traffic-eng tunnels\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng reserved-for-binding\\n##\\n\\nThe `mpls te reserved-for-binding` command in Huawei specifies that the TE tunnel is reserved for binding to other services (like L2VPN). In Cisco, the equivalent is to first enable MPLS TE globally (`mpls traffic-eng tunnels`), then configure the tunnel-specific reservation using `tunnel mpls traffic-eng reserved-for-binding`. \\n\\nNote: Cisco's exact implementation may vary slightly depending on IOS/IOS-XE version, and some contexts may require additional parameters. The above is the closest functional equivalent.\", 'trans_template': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng reserved-for-binding'}]]": 1,
